    "## DP Scalability Discussion (Deliverable 5)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "6ac08509",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# DP Scalability Discussion (Deliverable 5)\n",
    "print('\\n' + '=' * 60)\n",
    "print('DELIVERABLE 5: DP Scalability Discussion')\n",
    "print('=' * 60)\n",
    "print(\"\"\"\n",
    "DYNAMIC PROGRAMMING SCALABILITY ANALYSIS\n",
    "============================================================\n",
    "\n",
    "1. CURSE OF DIMENSIONALITY\n",
    "   |S| = (Grid Positions) × (Battery Levels) × 2^(Rescue Targets)\n",
    "   5×5, bat=15, 2 targets: 23 × 16 × 4 = 1,472 states → DP runs in seconds ✅\n",
    "   10×10, bat=20, 5 targets: ~672,000 states → ⚠️ Minutes\n",
    "   20×20, bat=30, 10 targets: ~12.6B states → ❌ Impossible\n",
    "   Each added rescue target doubles the state space.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. WHY DP BECOMES IMPRACTICAL\n",
    "   a) Must store V(s) for EVERY state (memory)\n",
    "   b) Each sweep: O(|S|×|A|) computations\n",
    "   c) Requires EXACT P(s'|s,a) — often unknown in practice\n",
    "\n",
    "3. HOW DEEP RL METHODS COULD HELP\n",
    "   DQN: neural net approximates Q(s,a), learns from sampled transitions\n",
    "   PPO/A3C: policy gradient, stable for complex environments\n",
    "   Key advantage — GENERALIZATION: similar states share value estimates\n",
    "\n",
    "4. REAL-WORLD DRONE CHALLENGES BEYOND THIS GRID\n",
    "   - Continuous 3D state space (position, velocity, orientation)\n",
